   "summary": "Unlock these with Subscription As the political climate heats up ahead of next year’s Assembly polls in Punjab, the Bharatiya Janata Party (BJP), and the Shiromani Akali Dal (SAD) are set to hold a joint press conference in New Delhi today (November 24, 2026).",
   "summary_zh": "订阅即可解锁。随着明年旁遮普邦议会选举前的政治局势升温，印度人民党（BJP）和锡龙尼阿卡利党（SAD）定于今天（2026年11月24日）在新德里举行联合新闻发布会。",
   "summarySource": "fulltext",
   "body": "Account subscription benefits alongside Premium Stories, Editorials, Opinions and more. Unlock these with Subscription As the political climate heats up ahead of next year’s Assembly polls in Punjab, the Bharatiya Janata Party (BJP), and the Shiromani Akali Dal (SAD) are set to hold a joint press conference in New Delhi today (November 24, 2026). Union Home Minister Amit Shah, BJP national president Nitin Nabin, and SAD president Sukhbir Singh Badal will address the meeting at the BJP headquarters in New Delhi at 1 p.m. While no further information is available on the subject of the presser, it is widely anticipated that the two parties will provide details on the alliance for the Punjab Assembly polls. Negotiations on seat-sharing between the BJP and the SAD for the 2027 Punjab Assembly polls have been underway. The SAD had snapped its 24-year-old alliance with the BJP in September 2020 over the now-scrapped three farm laws. Earlier, Lok Sabha MP Harsimrat Kaur Badal was also part of the Modi government as a Minister with Cabinet rank. Last month in Delhi, Shah had predicted an NDA government in Punjab in 2027. When he came to Chandigarh on November 1, he again said an NDA government will be formed in the border State. The statement reignited the buzz about a possible SAD-BJP alliance. On November 2, when SAD president Badal was asked about the possibility of a re-alliance, he gave a guarded response to speculation, saying, \"You will come to know in a few days as to what will happen\". He reiterated that, except for the Congress and the AAP, he is open to an alliance with other parties which accept his party's philosophy. In the 2022 Assembly polls, the BJP won two seats, and the SAD could register victories in three Assembly segments out of a total of 117. Prior to the end of the electoral alliance, both the Akalis and the BJP used to contest together. As per the previous pact in the Assembly polls, the BJP used to contest 23 seats and the SAD 94 Assembly seats. In parliamentary polls, the SAD used to fight for 10 seats and the BJP the remaining three. Comments have to be in English, and in full sentences. They cannot be abusive or personal. Please abide by our community guidelines for posting your comments. We have migrated to a new commenting platform. If you are already a registered user of The Hindu and logged in, you may continue to engage with our articles. If you do not have an account please register and login to post comments. Users can access their older comments by logging into their accounts on Vuukle",
