   "title": "Intelligence rift with US could become security crisis if not resolved quickly, lawmaker warns",
   "url": "https://www.koreatimes.co.kr/foreignaffairs/20261123/intelligence-rift-with-us-could-become-security-crisis-if-not-resolved-quickly-lawmaker-warns",
   "domain": "koreatimes.co.kr",
   "source": "koreatimes.co.kr",
   "published": "Mon, 23 Nov 2026 10:27:02 GMT",
   "_dt": 1795429622.0,
   "ageDays": 0.86,
   "summary": "“Korea is currently experiencing dissonance with the U.S. in intelligence cooperation, which could escalate into a grave security crisis if not restored quickly,” Rep. Song Seog-jun of the main opposition People Power Party said during an interview with The Korea Times, Wednesday, noting that South Korea still relies heavily on military intelligence from both the U.S. and Japan.",
   "summary_zh": "主要反对党国民力量党议员宋锡俊周三接受《韩国时报》采访时表示：“韩国目前在与美国的情报合作方面存在不协调，如果不迅速恢复合作，可能升级为严重的安全危机。”他指出，韩国目前仍高度依赖美国和日本提供的军事情报。",
   "summarySource": "fulltext",
   "body": "“Korea is currently experiencing dissonance with the U.S. in intelligence cooperation, which could escalate into a grave security crisis if not restored quickly,” Rep. Song Seog-jun of the main opposition People Power Party said during an interview with The Korea Times, Wednesday, noting that South Korea still relies heavily on military intelligence from both the U.S. and Japan. “We must solidify ties with our traditional allies so that South Korea can effectively leverage the mature and robust national strength it has built,” he said, adding that stronger alliances could compel North Korea, China and Russia to offer concessions. His remarks came amid lingering friction in Seoul-Washington security and trade ties, ranging from delays in investment negotiations to restrictions on intelligence sharing about North Korea, even as ties among Pyongyang, Beijing and Moscow continue to deepen. Song argued that South Korea’s international standing is stronger than ever and that it is time for Seoul to act accordingly. “During the period when we had lost our national sovereignty, we had no choice but to simply go along with the decisions of major powers, but things are different now,” the lawmaker said. \"Our manufacturing capabilities have grown across the board, from advanced semiconductors to defense production and our shipbuilding technology is world-class. All of this reflects how much stronger we’ve become as a country and how much better equipped we are to respond in a crisis.\" Referencing Seo Hui, a 10th-century civil official who secured territorial concessions from the Khitan empire through negotiation rather than battle, Song said South Korea has a distinctive capacity for cooperation and must channel it into proactive diplomatic leadership. “South Korea has a distinctive capacity for conciliation — what I call a spirit of mutual prosperity and harmony. Now is the time to channel that strength into proactive, assertive diplomatic leadership, taking the initiative to mediate competing national interests and guide this complex landscape toward a positive outcome,” he said. He criticized the Lee Jae Myung administration for imposing excessive regulations on businesses, which could undermine diplomatic potential. “These excessive corporate regulations could weaken companies and ultimately undermine South Korea’s national strength and long-term prospects,” Song said. “South Korea–China economic ties used to be remarkably strong. As the saying ‘security with the U.S., economy with China’ suggests, China served as our largest export market and a mutually complementary trading partner,” he said. Asked how to improve inter-Korean relations, Song urged patience and consistency. “It may sound overly idealistic at a moment like this, but we must stay true to fundamentals — engaging North Korea consistently, patiently and unwaveringly while offering a shared vision for the future,” he said, adding the two Koreas have long-standing principles of mutual non-aggression, exchange and trust-building. He said advances in digital technology now offer new ways to reach the North Korean public directly. “While outreach to the North once relied mainly on loudspeaker broadcasts and leaflets, advancing digital technologies and modern communications will increasingly allow us to share information about a better future and I believe the North Korean people will awaken,” he said, adding that North Korea cannot remain isolated indefinitely",
